WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.890
- One of the problems with urban planning

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.840
is that change is difficult.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.940
Most towns are built around cars.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.080
Many of them want to switch

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.750
to something that's more
transit-friendly and walkable,

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.510
but it's a very slow
and difficult process.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.000
But what if the car never arrived?

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.760
What if your town was so
inaccessible for so long

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.030
that, by the time it became
possible for cars to drive in,

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.190
the people decided they didn't want them?

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.720
This is Zermatt, a ski resort in the Alps
in southern Switzerland,

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.740
and this is the
end of the valley.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.930
There's no easy way past those mountains.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.460
You can't drive in here.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.100
There is a road, but it's
narrow and twisting,

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.960
and only open if you
get special permission

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.730
and pay quite a large fee.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.550
And even then, you can't
get into the town proper.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.250
Instead, the next town
over has a lot of parking

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.040
and a train to shuttle
tourists back and forth.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.080
It used to be that the only vehicles here

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.340
were horse-drawn carriages.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.010
But in the 1980s, Zermatt modernized,

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.498
jumping straight past petrol cars
and internal combustion engines,

00:00:56.498 --> 00:00:58.560
and going fully electric,

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.260
with some very specific requirements.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.240
- We don't have private cars.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.049
The hoteliers and all the builders
and all the taxis of course,

00:01:07.049 --> 00:01:10.920
can't go just by bicycle,
so they ask.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.430
The local government
makes the decision.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:18.307
The business people have
to send us the paper:

00:01:18.307 --> 00:01:20.730
"Why you need a vehicle?"

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:22.560
And then, we have this checklist.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.940
We give them permission,
maybe for three years.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.970
And if the business is running,
he can keep it.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:34.970
But if you are a person like...
Tom Scott, why'd you need one?

00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:41.100
"Yeah, I live up here.
It's very difficult to go up there."

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.550
Then we would say, "Sorry,
you can take a taxi."

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.920
And all the deliverers,
they have a car,

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.500
but not the shops, also
not the restaurants.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:55.860
We are really strict that, since 20 years,
we have around 520 vehicles.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.410
- If you grew up in Britain
around the same time as I did,

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.260
you might be thinking,
"Those are milk floats".

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.750
And yes, it's basically
the same technology:

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.990
a battery electric vehicle
with a very long service life,

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.480
designed for decades of low speed use,

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.130
to be fixed easily if it breaks,

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.660
and to make a minimum of noise.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.549
In the 20th century, British dairies
used fleets of vehicles like that

00:02:16.549 --> 00:02:19.680
for delivering bottles of milk
to millions of homes every morning.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.170
Which seems weird these days,
but then,

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.760
so does a lot of the 20th century.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.550
Zermatt here used these
vehicles for everything.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:29.807
Taxis are the most obvious one,
but buses, trucks, the police car; 

00:02:29.807 --> 00:02:30.930
they all looked like that,

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.439
and many of them were designed
and built locally

00:02:33.439 --> 00:02:35.430
by a company just over there

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.251
that started out with
horse-drawn taxis.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:42.300
- It took me a while to notice something
as I walked around Zermatt.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.650
In every city, in every town,

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:47.023
there is a constant quiet roar
of traffic noise from somewhere, 

00:04:47.023 --> 00:04:48.570
even if it's off in the distance.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.970
And here, there just isn't.
It's missing.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.320
And that's really obvious at night,

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.120
but right now, of course,
that's very difficult

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.130
to demonstrate because
there's construction,

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:57.480
there's the rush of the river

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.490
that's really audible
from this vantage point;

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.710
it's carrying all the glacier-melt
water down the valley.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.570
There are helicopters and trains,

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:06.120
but at night when everything's calm,

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:07.260
there's no traffic noise.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.806
And while I know it's a one-off,

00:05:08.806 --> 00:05:09.688
and I know it only works because

00:05:09.688 --> 00:05:13.662
this is a small, rich and very expensive
ski resort with a weird history 

00:05:13.662 --> 00:05:16.890
that's outsourced its parking
to the next town over...!

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.140
I can't help thinking that it'd be nice

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.930
if more towns and cities
sounded like that.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:23.940
- Everybody knows the rules.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:25.777
The children grow up with this.

00:05:25.777 --> 00:05:28.380
"Can I have a car?"
"Are you crazy?"

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:31.500
So, it's in the mind from the locals.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.397
When I tell people from Switzerland,

00:05:33.397 --> 00:05:36.450
"We are living in a town
without petrol cars,"

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.070
they say, "How you manage?"

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:44.910
"By bicycle, or by foot, or by bus?"
"Ah. Huh."

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:47.580
So, they can't believe it.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:51.723
But if you are in this system,
then it's so normal.

